  "query_preview": "..High pressure remains centered over New Mexico for \nthe next few days, before wandering westward later this week and \ninto next weekend. The position of the high and southeasterly \nsteering flow has allowed Monsoonal moisture to stay in place and \ncurrent PW values have not really shown any appreciable drying \ntrends, as of yet.\n\nThe strength of the ridge peaks today, before actually weakening \never so slightly Tuesday and Wednesday, then strengthening again as \nit moves westward later this week. The brief weakening of the high \nmay allow some of the weak capping over our area to relax Tuesday \nand Wednesday, which may ramp up activity compared to today. As the \nhigh retrogrades later this week, we expect drier air and stronger \ncapping to develop, which will limit activity, especially north of \nthe I-40 corridor.\n\nFor today, we expect a similar day to Sunday. Thunderstorms will \ninitiate over the higher terrain of the Mogollon Rim and then move \nnorthwest. Outflows may push some activity off the Mogollon Rim and \ninto eastern Yavapai County later in the afternoon. Western Yavapai \nCounty and the zones NE of the Mogollon Rim will be comparatively \nquiet.\n\nAs the ridge strengthens later this week, expect temperatures to \nrise, especially as cloud cover and rain-cooled air becomes more \nscarce. It is likely that temperatures will reach toward the warmest \nso far this year by next weekend. But, for now, we will stay free of \nheadlines as anticipated convective activity should keep the lower \nelevations just under criteria for a few days.",
